 the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}})≈{54}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535405 false] [17592186279151 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064104/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535405 true] [17592186279151 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535405 false] [17592186299797 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨3,3,-5⟩,\\,q=⟨4,2,-3⟩,\\,r=⟨7,5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064104/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨7,5,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535405 true] [17592186299797 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨3,3,-5⟩,\\,q=⟨4,2,-3⟩,\\,r